prop title = "Posterior Visualizer"
prop date = "2026-09-04"
Bayes' rule is four symbols and a horizontal line, and I have never once found
that enough to see what it *does*. So here is the whole thing taken apart: a
coin with unknown bias $\theta$, a handful of flips, and every term of the rule
laid out in the order you would actually compute it.

Play with it. Drag $\theta$ and watch the likelihood row rewrite itself. Flip a
coin from heads to tails and watch the posterior slide left. Widen the prior and
watch the data take over.

Three things are worth noticing:

- The **likelihood** is not a distribution over $\theta$. It is a number you get
  by picking a $\theta$ and asking how well it explains the flips you have. That
  is why it is computed here rather than set.
- The **evidence** $p(X)$ is one number. It does not depend on $\theta$, so
  dividing by it can only rescale the curve — it can never move the peak. All of
  the *shape* of the posterior comes from prior $\times$ likelihood.
- With a Beta prior the posterior is Beta again, and the update is just
  counting: $\alpha$ gains a head, $\beta$ gains a tail. Conjugacy is the reason
  the picture stays this clean.

<Posterior />

The prior here is $\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha, \beta)$ because it is the natural
distribution over a probability. It lives on $[0, 1]$, it is flat when
$\alpha = \beta = 1$, and it can be pushed toward either end or squeezed toward
the middle with two knobs. A Gaussian cannot do that without leaking probability
past the ends of the interval.

The likelihood assumes the flips are independent given $\theta$, so it is the
product $\theta^h (1-\theta)^t$ over the $h$ heads and $t$ tails. Note that $X$
is the *sequence* of flips, not the count, so there is no binomial coefficient
out front. It would cancel in the ratio anyway.

The evidence is the integral of that product against the prior, and for a Beta
prior it has a closed form:

$$
p(X) = \int_0^1 \theta^{h}(1-\theta)^{t}\, p(\theta)\, \mathrm{d}\theta
     = \frac{B(\alpha + h,\, \beta + t)}{B(\alpha,\, \beta)}
$$

so nothing on this page is a numerical approximation. Put those together and the
posterior is $\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha + h,\, \beta + t)$ — the same curve, redrawn
with the counts folded in.
